{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794344389,"nonce":"tvqmdGKgFOuP6TAl7/5Z0HVJhrk=","title":"Image border colour is interpolated as a float and corrupts for some colours","message":"The image shader carries the border colour as four bytes packed into the bits of `params.w`, and `VertexOutput.params` is interpolated, not `@interpolate(flat)`. A packed colour whose bits are a NaN or a denormal does not survive the GPU: `(1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 1.0)` packs to 0xFF8000FF, a signalling NaN, and draws a border of the wrong colour and alpha (the red and green bytes change, the alpha drops); colours with a zero red byte and a small green byte are denormals and may flush to zero. A colour such as `(0.25, 0.5, 0.0, 1.0)` is an ordinary float and is fine.\n\nReproducer: draw `GpuImageInstance::new(..).with_border_radius(6.0).with_image_border(2.0, 1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 1.0)` through `sdf_russl::image` and read a pixel on the border ring: it is not the orange asked for.\n\nFix direction: pass the border colour as a flat `Vec4` or `u32` varying (the colour channels travel in a separate attribute or the packed word is a flat `u32` varying read in the fragment stage). The vertex attribute is a Float32x4 in `GpuImageInstance::params` and its layout is shared with the pipeline setup, so it needs a Uint32 attribute or a flat float varying that is bit-cast in the fragment stage. Flat does not interpolate, so the bits arrive unchanged. Then add the colour back to `image_parity.rs`, which uses a finite one for now.","files":null}]}